= General Policy and Principles

== Welcome to Noice & Toit LLP

At Noice & Toit LLP, our reputation rests on delivering rigorous professional counsel, high technical standards, and unquestioned integrity. As a global partnership with offices across London, Berlin, New York, Zurich, and Singapore, our professionals regularly travel to client sites, courtroom venues, research facilities, and international conferences.

This Expense Policy Handbook establishes a clear, practical framework for how we manage, substantiate, and reimburse professional business expenditures. It applies to every partner, associate, analyst, business operations specialist, intern, and retained contractor across all offices and subsidiaries.

We treat company money as our collective partnership capital. Our policy is grounded in trust, simplicity, and personal responsibility. We do not micro-manage legitimate work travel, but we expect all team members to exercise sound judgment, prudence, and fair dealing.

== The Noice & Toit Operating Philosophy

Our core philosophy is simple: when spending company funds or incurring expenses that will be billed to clients, act as a prudent steward. Ask yourself whether an independent observer or client audit committee would consider the expenditure reasonable, necessary, and proportionate.

#card-do("Spend partnership funds with care")[
  Book sensible itineraries, preserve original itemized documentation, and submit claims promptly at the end of each trip. When in doubt about whether an unusual expenditure is allowable, discuss it with your practice group leader before committing funds.
]

#card-dont("Treat expense accounts as personal perquisites")[
  Never use company credit cards, partner accounts, or expense reports for personal living costs, leisure items, or family obligations. Keep your personal and firm finances strictly separate at all times.
]

== Roles and Administrative Responsibilities

Effective expense management is a shared responsibility across three key groups within the firm:

=== The Traveler or Incurring Employee
- Understand and follow the guidelines outlined in this handbook before making travel commitments.
- Use our approved corporate booking channels and preferred hotel partners whenever available.
- Collect and digitize complete, itemized tax invoices and folios at the point of sale.
- File well-organized expense reports within thirty days of incurring the cost.
- Promptly resolve inquiries or deduction notices raised by the Finance team.

=== The Approving Manager or Practice Leader
- Confirm that every expense claimed serves an authentic business purpose connected to a project, client matter, or approved firm initiative.
- Verify that hotel room rates, meal totals, and transportation choices fall within policy caps.
- Ensure that billable expenses match client engagement letters and billing guidelines.
- Reject or adjust unsupported line items before approving reports in the portal.

=== Global Finance and People Operations
- Maintain and publish updated mileage rates, hotel city tiers, and foreign exchange reference schedules.
- Audit submissions for regulatory compliance, tax deductibility, and internal policy conformance.
- Disburse approved reimbursements into employee payroll accounts on scheduled bi-weekly cycles.
- Manage corporate card issuance, credit limits, and banking vendor relationships.

== Ethical Standards and Anti-Corruption Compliance

Noice & Toit LLP adheres to strict international legal standards, including the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act (FCPA), the UK Bribery Act, and applicable European anti-corruption directives.

#card-do("Comply with anti-bribery regulations")[
  Ensure all client hospitality, gifts, and business entertainment are transparent, modest, and properly documented with full attendee details. Hospitality must never be offered to influence official actions or obtain improper commercial advantages.
]

#card-dont("Provide entertainment to public officials without legal clearance")[
  Never purchase meals, travel, gifts, or entertainment for government officials, regulatory authorities, or state-owned enterprise representatives without advance written approval from General Counsel.
]

== Regular Audits, Deductions, and Dispute Resolution

Finance reviews all expense reports submitted through the firm portal. The majority of submissions pass through automated validation and routine manager review, but Finance also conducts random and targeted audits.

#card-do("Retain digital copies of receipts")[
  Keep digital copies or photos of your submitted receipts for at least twelve months after report settlement. Tax authorities or client auditors frequently request historical supporting documentation during retrospective reviews.
]

#card-dont("Submit claims with missing or ambiguous details")[
  Do not submit generic descriptions like "business dinner" or "travel expenses". State the client code, project milestone, or administrative purpose clearly to avoid unnecessary audit delays.
]

=== Clawback Rights and Disciplinary Procedures
If an audit reveals that an unapproved, exaggerated, or prohibited expense was paid out in error, Finance will notify the employee and offset the amount against the next payroll disbursement. Intentional falsification of expense reports constitutes serious misconduct and will result in disciplinary action up to and including termination of employment or dissolution of partnership status.

== Governance, Interpretation, and Precedence

This handbook is maintained by the Policy Stewardship Committee, a standing body chaired by the Chief Operating Officer with representation from Global Finance, People Operations, General Counsel, and the Regional Practice Directors. The Committee meets quarterly to ratify amendments, publish clarifications, and retire obsolete guidance. No local office, practice group, or client team may unilaterally amend, dilute, or restate the provisions of this handbook. Proposed changes are submitted in writing to the Committee secretary, reviewed at the next scheduled meeting, and take effect only upon publication through the firm's official policy channel.

=== Binding Effect and Order of Precedence

The numbered chapters of this handbook constitute the sole binding source of expense policy for the firm. The limits, caps, and allowances stated in those chapters are firm-wide commitments and are applied uniformly across all offices. Supplemental programs — sustainability initiatives, well-being schemes, local office guidance, vendor loyalty arrangements, or client-funded hospitality frameworks — may encourage economical behavior and may offer non-monetary recognition, but they never increase, extend, or substitute for any binding limit stated in the numbered chapters. Where a supplemental document appears to conflict with this handbook, this handbook prevails, and the supplemental document is read as persuasive background only.

Interpretation questions are resolved by the following order of authority: first, the express text of the numbered chapters; second, published clarifications issued by the Policy Stewardship Committee; third, the interpretive notes of the Records Office. Informal advice from colleagues, vendor documentation, or precedent from individual audit outcomes do not constitute authoritative interpretation. A traveler who relies on informal advice does so at their own risk, and an adverse audit outcome cannot be set aside on the ground that a colleague sanctioned the expenditure verbally.

Where a provision is genuinely ambiguous, the Records Office drafts a formal clarification, General Counsel reviews it for legal consistency, and the Committee ratifies it before publication. Until a clarification is published, travelers should choose the more conservative reading and document the reasoning in their claim notes.

=== Scope: Subsidiaries, Affiliates, Secondees, and Contractors

This handbook applies in full to all partners, salaried employees, trainees, and interns of Noice & Toit LLP and its wholly owned subsidiaries, regardless of contracting entity or payroll location. It applies to secondees hosted by the firm for the duration of the secondment, except where the seconding organization's terms expressly and unavoidably differ, in which case the more restrictive provision governs. Retained contractors and professional service vendors operating under a firm engagement letter may recover only those expenditures expressly designated as reimbursable in that engagement letter; nothing in this handbook creates a reimbursement entitlement for contractors beyond the letter's terms.

Joint ventures, minority-held affiliates, and alumni associations are outside the scope of this handbook unless a written cost-sharing agreement designates this handbook as the controlling document. The Travel Desk maintains the register of entities and engagement types to which the handbook applies and answers scope questions raised before a booking is made.

