== Audit Philosophy and Sampling Methodology

The firm's audit posture is preventive rather than adversarial. Automated validation intercepts the majority of documentation defects at submission, so human review concentrates on judgment questions: business purpose, proportionality, and the quality of supporting narratives. The Audit Committee approves the audit methodology annually, and Finance reports aggregate findings to the partnership each financial year.

Finance operates three audit streams. *Routine review* covers a rolling random sample of all submitted reports, selected by the portal without human involvement, so that every submission carries an equal chance of review regardless of amount or claimant. *Targeted review* concentrates on categories with historically elevated correction rates, vendor anomalies flagged by the corporate card provider, and client-mandated audits under engagement letters. *For-cause review* is opened when a specific concern is raised by a manager, a client auditor, or an external authority. Sampling intensity is calibrated by the Audit Committee and adjusted annually; the selection criteria are not disclosed in advance, and no claimant can predict or request exemption from any stream.

An audit finding is a documented conclusion that an expenditure lacked adequate support, fell outside a binding provision, or served no authentic business purpose. First-time findings of an administrative nature are corrected through resubmission and coaching. Repeated findings, or any finding involving intent, are referred by the Audit Committee to People Operations under the disciplinary provisions stated earlier in this handbook.

== Records Stewardship and Data Privacy

The Records Office owns the expense archive on behalf of the partnership. Every submitted claim, receipt, approval, and audit note is retained according to the firm's retention schedule, which distinguishes between the retention required by tax authorities in each jurisdiction, the retention required by client engagement letters, and the firm's own longer internal schedule. Where multiple schedules apply to the same record, the longest applicable retention governs. Travelers should not maintain private shadow archives of firm expenses; the portal archive is authoritative, searchable, and accessible to the traveler for their own claims.

Expense records contain personal data and are processed under the firm's privacy framework and applicable data protection law. Access is restricted on a need-to-know basis: approvers see their own reporting lines, Finance reviewers see what validation requires, and audit teams see what the Audit Committee-approved methodology requires. Requests by third parties — client auditors, tax authorities, or law enforcement — for access to expense records are routed exclusively through General Counsel. Travelers may inspect their own records at any time and may request correction of factual errors in their claims through the Records Office.

== Anti-Bribery Case Narratives

The following scenarios describe how the firm's anti-corruption standards operate in practice. They are binding guidance on the conduct they describe.

*Authority engagement at client venues.* A regulatory counsel at a state financial authority attends a closing dinner hosted by the client, and the engagement partner is asked to settle the authority's share of the bill as a courtesy. Any charge incurred for the benefit of a public official requires advance written clearance from General Counsel, regardless of amount, venue, or occasion. The engagement partner should decline the courtesy, record the interaction in the matter file, and raise the request with General Counsel before any commitment is made.

*Facilitation pressure.* A traveler at a foreign border is told that a cash "handling fee" will speed the release of demonstration equipment. Facilitation payments are prohibited without exception, even where they are locally customary and even where refusal causes delay. The traveler should decline, note the incident, and report it to the Travel Desk, which maintains a log used in vendor-country risk reviews.

*Hospitality as inducement.* A supplier offers a partner complimentary attendance at an invitation-only industry retreat during contract renewal negotiations. Hospitality accepted during a live commercial decision creates the appearance of inducement. The offer should be referred to General Counsel before acceptance, and attendance, if cleared, must be documented with attendee identities, business purpose, and the relationship between the parties.

== Dispute and Clarification Workflow

A traveler who disagrees with a Finance deduction may open a dispute in the portal within the response window stated on the deduction notice. Disputes are reviewed in three steps: first by the original Finance reviewer, second by the Finance team lead, and third, at the traveler's written request, by the practice director together with the Policy Stewardship Committee's secretariat. Each step is documented in the portal, and the outcome at each step is stated with reasons. Disputes do not delay unrelated claims, and a pending dispute never authorizes resubmission of the same expenditure as a new claim.

Clarification requests — questions about how a provision applies to a situation the handbook does not expressly address — follow a separate route. The traveler or approver submits the facts, the provision in question, and the proposed reading to the Records Office. The Records Office drafts a written clarification, General Counsel reviews it, and ratified clarifications are published to all offices so that interpretation remains uniform firm-wide. Travelers may rely on a published clarification until it is withdrawn or amended.

